Data-flow patterns that turned out to be right in more than one place. Proven
useful, therefore worth reaching for first the next time something looks similar.

--

## Generate, then view. Never both at once.

The simulation produces data; something else draws it. The headless runner
produces a match; something else reports on it. The batch runner produces ten
thousand lines; something else reads them. The Markdown is the data; the HTML is
a view of it.

Every time this split exists, a bug has a side, and the search space halves. Every
time it does not, a wrong pixel and a wrong rule look identical.

## Copy at the boundary. Never hold a reference across it.

Every body in this game carries its own copy of what modifies it. Nothing in the
swing path dereferences a lane, a tower, or a team record to find out how strong
something is. **Copy at the boundary between something that changes rarely and
something that is read constantly**, and then let the fast side touch only itself.

The design consequence -- a placement takes effect one wave later -- was an accident
of the performance decision and turned out to be the mechanism that makes
placements worth arguing about. Look for that. Fast and right often agree.

### And when the boundary moves, recopy. Do not adjust.

An earlier version of this note said "stamp once, read many," with a carve-out
for towers, which were read live because they are rare and long-lived. That
carve-out was withdrawn: it put a reference back into the hot path to buy
something that a sweep buys for free.

The rule that replaced it: **when a source changes, clear every copy downstream
of it and rebuild from the current truth.** Not patch, not apply a delta --
clear and rebuild. An incremental adjustment has to know what changed, apply the
difference, and be right about the order things happened in; a rebuild reads what
is true now and writes it. The first is faster and drifts. The second is slower
and cannot.

It is only affordable because the sources change rarely -- a placement, a boon
pick -- and the copies downstream of any one source are few. Check that before
reaching for this, because the same pattern over a source that changes every tick
is the slowest thing in the program.

The exception is deliberate and worth stating wherever it appears: **some copies
are never corrected on purpose.** A wave unit keeps what it was born with even
after the lane changes, because the delay is the mechanic. A stale copy that
somebody chose is a feature; a stale copy that nobody chose is the bug this
pattern exists to prevent, and the two look identical in the source. Comment
both.

## A small integer beats a measurement.

Milestones, not distances. Push depth, not position. Ticks, not seconds. Every
time this design reached for a real-valued measurement it got the wrong answer to
a question about *progress*, and every time it reached for an ordinal it got the
right one.

The general shape: if the question is "which of these is further along," the
answer is an index into a list somebody authored, not arithmetic on coordinates.

## Dispatch tables, not branches.

Soldier states, command verbs, upgrade behaviours, abilities, phases, and the
tick order itself. In every case the value already knows what it means, and the
table says what knowing that means. Adding a case is adding a row.

The real payoff is not speed. It is that the **order of the simulation became a
piece of readable data** instead of something buried in a function body.

## Buffer, then resolve.

Damage is written into an array and applied in a second pass. This bought three
things at once: simultaneous deaths that are fair, a pass that can be sliced
across a thread pool, and an ordering that does not depend on which slot happened
to be free. Whenever two things must be able to happen at the same instant, write
both down first and settle them afterwards.

## Zero is a sentinel. Nil is a question.

Nothing in the simulation is ever nil. Empty means the integer zero, which has a
meaning. A nil check asks whether some earlier code did its job, and that question
belongs in a validator that runs once, not in a loop that runs a thousand times a
tick.

## Refuse loudly, in words.

Every rejected command carries a reason, and the reason is a sentence. This is
not politeness -- it is the game's only teaching mechanism, because nobody reads a
rules screen. The same applies to every validator in the project: a failure names
what was missing and where, and never substitutes a default.

## Make the tool, not the thing.

The map is generated, never hand-edited. The documentation's HTML is generated.
The demos are regenerated. Anything hand-tweaked is a thing nobody dares change
afterwards.
